title: A register with reset
mode: vhdl
chapter: Memory
check: behaves
cycles: 150
random: rst=0.1 load=0.3
---- intro
Registers usually have both: `rst` sets them to zero, and `load` puts a new value in. When both are on, reset wins. An `elsif` says it in order:

```
if rst = '1' then
    q <= (others => '0');
elsif load = '1' then
    q <= d;
end if;
```

`(others => '0')` means "every bit 0", whatever the width.
---- task
At each rising edge: `q` becomes all zeros if `rst` is 1; else it takes `d` if `load` is 1; else it keeps its value.
---- start
library ieee;
use ieee.std_logic_1164.all;

entity reg4_rst is
  port(
    clk  : in  std_logic;
    rst  : in  std_logic;
    load : in  std_logic;
    d    : in  std_logic_vector(3 downto 0);
    q    : out std_logic_vector(3 downto 0)
  );
end reg4_rst;

architecture rtl of reg4_rst is
begin
    -- write it here
end rtl;
---- answer
library ieee;
use ieee.std_logic_1164.all;

entity reg4_rst is
  port(
    clk  : in  std_logic;
    rst  : in  std_logic;
    load : in  std_logic;
    d    : in  std_logic_vector(3 downto 0);
    q    : out std_logic_vector(3 downto 0)
  );
end reg4_rst;

architecture rtl of reg4_rst is
begin
    process (clk)
    begin
        if rising_edge(clk) then
            if rst = '1' then
                q <= (others => '0');
            elsif load = '1' then
                q <= d;
            end if;
        end if;
    end process;
end rtl;
---- hint1
Inside the clock edge: `if rst = '1' then ... elsif load = '1' then ... end if;`
---- hint2
`if rst = '1' then q <= (others => '0'); elsif load = '1' then q <= d; end if;`
---- solution
```
process (clk)
begin
    if rising_edge(clk) then
        if rst = '1' then
            q <= (others => '0');
        elsif load = '1' then
            q <= d;
        end if;
    end if;
end process;
```
---- done
The order of `if` and `elsif` is the priority: whatever is checked first wins. In hardware that is one multiplexer in front of another.
